    </tbody>
  </table>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Renewals.</strong> The House bill gives a 1.4% increase for voucher renewals; the budget asked for 1.7% (CRS).</li>
    <li><strong>Administrative fees.</strong> The House bill gives $536 million less than FY2026.</li>
    <li><strong>Turnover.</strong> The budget asked that housing authorities stop reissuing vouchers when a family leaves, except in some cases. The House bill does not include this (CRS).</li>
    <li><strong>HUD as a whole.</strong> The budget asked for $64.9 billion in net new HUD funding, 16.0% less than FY2026 (CRS).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="hap">Can a housing authority stop HAP payments?</h2>
  <p>
    A housing authority can end a HAP contract for lack of money, but only under HUD rules. 24 CFR 982.454 says: "The PHA
    may terminate the HAP contract if the PHA determines, in accordance with HUD requirements, that funding under the
    consolidated ACC is insufficient to support continued assistance for families in the program."
  </p>
  <p>
    HUD tries to prevent that step. Notice PIH 2026-12 sets aside "Category 1" shortfall money to prevent terminations, and
    calls that HUD's "highest priority". HUD would approve terminations only "in extremely rare circumstances". But the same
    notice says demand for shortfall money "likely will exceed available funds", and HUD's 2026 Budget Management Letter
    says HUD "cannot guarantee that funding will be available to resolve all shortfall needs".
  </p>
  <p>
    For CY2026, HUD estimated the national renewal proration at 100% (Notice PIH 2026-12). That means most housing
    authorities got their full renewal formula amount. A shortfall comes when a housing authority's costs grow faster
    than that amount.
  </p>

  <h2 id="shortfall">What a housing authority in shortfall may do</h2>
  <p>
    A housing authority in shortfall cuts costs before it cuts families. HUD's 2026 Budget Management Letter and Notice
    PIH 2026-12 list these steps:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Stop new vouchers.</strong> No new vouchers, except HUD-VASH and Foster Youth to Independence. HUD can also require a housing authority to rescind recently issued vouchers.</li>
    <li><strong>Lower payment standards.</strong> End exception payment standards and stay within 90% to 110% of the FMR. See the <a href="/guides/section-8-rent-limits/payment-standard-cuts-2026">payment standard cuts tracker</a> for housing authorities that did this.</li>
    <li><strong>Apply cuts faster.</strong> With good cause, ask HUD for a waiver to apply a payment standard cut to current contracts at once, or to go below 90% of the FMR.</li>
    <li><strong>Check rent reasonableness.</strong> Review how the housing authority approves rents and rent increases.</li>
    <li><strong>Pause project-based deals.</strong> No new project-based voucher agreements. Contracts already signed stay in force: the housing authority must still meet them.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A housing authority with a projected shortfall over 10% of its 2026 budget gets a confirmed shortfall email from HUD's
    Shortfall Prevention Team. HUD may keep 2026 shortfall agencies on cost cuts into 2027 (Notice PIH 2026-12).
  </p>

  <h2 id="ehv">Emergency Housing Vouchers end on 2026-12-31</h2>
  <p>
    Emergency Housing Vouchers (EHV) become regular vouchers by 2026-12-31. The stopgap law (section 153 of P.L. 119-103)
    lets HUD give a tenant protection voucher to each EHV family under lease on 2026-09-30, from $600.6 million (Notice
    PIH 2026-25).
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The family stays.</strong> No rescreening and no waiting list. The family "can remain in units under lease".</li>
    <li><strong>New rules.</strong> The housing authority's regular payment standard policy now applies to the family.</li>
    <li><strong>No reissue.</strong> When the family leaves the program, the voucher ends. It does not go to a new family.</li>
    <li><strong>Funding.</strong> HUD funds December 2026. Later months "are planned to be covered as renewals" and depend on future appropriations.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    What each housing authority told EHV owners, by date: <a href="/guides/emergency-housing-vouchers-landlords">What Landlords Get When Emergency Housing Vouchers End</a>.
  </p>

